        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Isometry.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Isometry.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-xor_preserves_distance",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE ISOMETRY: xoring both sides by the same key leaves the Hamming distance unchanged, for every pair and every key over the four-bit cube. This is the single fact the cipher, the strand and the code each hold a corner of."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(List.range 16).all (fun a => (List.range 16).all (fun b => (List.range 16).all (fun k => dist (lxor a k) (lxor b k) == dist a b)))"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem xor_preserves_distance : (List.range 16).all (fun a => (List.range 16).all (fun b => (List.range 16).all (fun k => dist (lxor a k) (lxor b k) == dist a b))) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-reuse_leaks_by_isometry",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "WHY KEY REUSE LEAKS, stated as the cause rather than the symptom: because the pad is an isometry, the distance between two ciphertexts EQUALS the distance between their plaintexts. An attacker with neither key nor message still reads a true fact about the messages. Cipher.lean seals that reuse leaks the plaintext XOR; this seals why it must."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(List.range 8).all (fun m1 => (List.range 8).all (fun m2 => (List.range 8).all (fun k => dist (lxor m1 k) (lxor m2 k) == dist m1 m2)))"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem reuse_leaks_by_isometry : (List.range 8).all (fun m1 => (List.range 8).all (fun m2 => (List.range 8).all (fun k => dist (lxor m1 k) (lxor m2 k) == dist m1 m2))) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-complement_flips_two",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "EVERY DNA BASE DIFFERS FROM ITS COMPLEMENT IN EXACTLY TWO BITS. A base is two bits, complementing is lxor with 3, and 3 has weight two — so the distance is two for all four bases. The strand's pairing is the pad's step, at width two."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 4).all (fun x => dist x (lxor x 3) == 2)) ∧ (pop 3 = 2)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem complement_flips_two : ((List.range 4).all (fun x => dist x (lxor x 3) == 2)) ∧ (pop 3 = 2) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-codon_flips_six",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND A CODON IS THREE BASES, SO SIX BITS: 4^3 = 64 = 2^6, and complementing a whole codon flips every one of the six — three bases at two bits each. The width scales with the word; the isometry does not change."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((4:Nat)^3 = 64) ∧ ((2:Nat)^6 = 64) ∧ (3 * 2 = 6) ∧ (pop 63 = 6)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem codon_flips_six : ((4:Nat)^3 = 64) ∧ ((2:Nat)^6 = 64) ∧ (3 * 2 = 6) ∧ (pop 63 = 6) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-distance_is_symmetric",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE DISTANCE IS A METRIC. Both halves on the line, so the second is discharged where it is claimed rather than assumed from the first."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(List.range 16).all (fun a => (List.range 16).all (fun b => (dist a b == dist b a) && ((dist a b == 0) == (a == b))))"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem distance_is_symmetric : (List.range 16).all (fun a => (List.range 16).all (fun b => (dist a b == dist b a) && ((dist a b == 0) == (a == b)))) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-isometry_bounds_correction",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND WHY A CODE CORRECTS AT ALL: correction depends only on distance, which the isometry preserves, so the decoder's geometry survives encoding. At distance three a decoder corrects one error and detects two — (3−1)/2 = 1 and 3−1 = 2 — and it cannot correct two, which the line proves rather than leaves implied."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((3 - 1) / 2 = 1) ∧ (3 - 1 = 2) ∧ ((3 - 1) / 2 ≠ 2)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem isometry_bounds_correction : ((3 - 1) / 2 = 1) ∧ (3 - 1 = 2) ∧ ((3 - 1) / 2 ≠ 2) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
